<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Banu Jami emirate of Gabes</h1><p class="st-meta">1097 CE – 1160 CE (approx.) · capital Gabes</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Hilalian Arab dynasty that ruled the port of Gabès in southern Tunisia during the breakup of Zirid Ifriqiya.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hilal">Banu Hilal</a> invasions of the 1050s shattered the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zirid-emirate">Zirid emirate</a>, local lords took control of the cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a>. Around 1097 the Banu <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jami">Jami</a>, a family of the Riyah, an Arab tribe of the Banu Hilal, established themselves as rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabes">Gabès</a> on the gulf of the same name.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Little is known in detail. The Banu <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jami">Jami</a> ruled the town and its coastal oasis, with its date palms and irrigated gardens, relying on Arab tribal support. Around 1117 the emir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafi-ibn-makkan">Rafi ibn Makkan</a> quarrelled with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zirid-dynasty">Zirids of Mahdia</a> over his merchant ship and sought help from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-ii-of-sicily">Roger II of Sicily</a>, an early step in Norman intervention in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabes">Gabès</a> came under Norman influence in the 1140s, and in 1160 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-mumin">Abd al-Mu'min</a> conquered it along with the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a>, ending the Banu Jami dynasty.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 1097 CE – 1160 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82